WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.438
This is Chapter 5: exposure and incidents.

4
00:00:09.438 --> 00:00:12.872
A vulnerability without an
owner is an inventory finding,

5
00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:15.876
and an incident without an
owner is a clock running.

6
00:00:15.876 --> 00:00:19.788
Both checks in this chapter ask
the same question: who has this?

7
00:00:19.788 --> 00:00:23.813
We'll start from the guide's queries
and Learn's incident list, and the

8
00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:26.976
government-cloud fact that
shapes the exposure gate,

9
00:00:26.976 --> 00:00:29.276
then read both checks and walk two labs.

10
00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:32.143
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved;

11
00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:35.840
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.090
Why put these two together?

13
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.028
Because both ask who has this.

14
00:00:43.028 --> 00:00:48.465
A vulnerability with no owner isn't just
unpatched; it's a gap in the inventory.

15
00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:52.715
And an incident with no owner is a clock
running against the severity table.

16
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:57.205
By the end of this chapter you can
route every exposed device to an owner,

17
00:00:57.205 --> 00:01:01.315
record Security Exposure Management
correctly for a GCC tenant,

18
00:01:01.315 --> 00:01:04.740
and list every incident that
broke the severity table.

19
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.311
It's for the developers, the vulnerability
manager who owns the exposure gate,

20
00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:12.820
and the security operations
lead who owns incidents.

21
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.758
One government-cloud fact
shapes the exposure gate.

22
00:01:19.758 --> 00:01:24.586
Learn says Microsoft Security Exposure
Management, with its attack paths,

23
00:01:24.586 --> 00:01:27.623
exposure graph and
critical asset management,

24
00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:31.595
is available in Public Cloud
only, not in US Government clouds.

25
00:01:31.595 --> 00:01:35.477
Defender Vulnerability
Management is available in GCC,

26
00:01:35.477 --> 00:01:38.120
core and premium, without two features.

27
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.170
DeviceInfo still carries exposure level,
internet facing, and asset value.

28
00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.933
And the vulnerability tables stay in
Defender; they aren't ingested into Sentinel.

29
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:52.700
So the guide's interim path prioritizes
by those columns and Vulnerability

30
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:58.270
Management's recommendations, with the Tier
0 inventory as the critical-asset list.

31
00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.020
What's left to you?

32
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.203
The exposure query
returns rows, not owners,

33
00:02:06.203 --> 00:02:11.508
so the kit matches each device's group
to an owner, and no owner is a finding.

34
00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:16.288
Not available is a fact, but a Zero
Trust Assessment test that needs Exposure

35
00:02:16.288 --> 00:02:20.461
Management would read as a failure,
so the kit writes a disposition:

36
00:02:20.461 --> 00:02:23.345
skipped, not applicable,
with the interim path.

37
00:02:23.345 --> 00:02:28.783
An incident export is a list, so each
incident is read against the severity table.

38
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:32.257
One filter misses an old
incident that's still open,

39
00:02:32.257 --> 00:02:34.657
so the kit reads two and merges them.

40
00:02:34.657 --> 00:02:38.587
And Graph doesn't record when
an incident was first assigned,

41
00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:43.260
so the kit reports unowned past its
window, and invents no duration.

42
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:52.316
The exposure check runs the guide's two
queries: high and critical vulnerabilities

43
00:02:52.316 --> 00:02:57.160
by software and recommended update,
and exposed or internet-facing devices.

44
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.954
It matches each exposed
device's group to a

45
00:02:59.954 --> 00:03:03.147
remediation owner from
the agency's owners file.

46
00:03:03.147 --> 00:03:08.385
A device with no owner is an inventory
finding, not just a vulnerability finding.

47
00:03:08.385 --> 00:03:11.535
And it writes the disposition
for the settings file's cloud.

48
00:03:11.535 --> 00:03:15.275
For GCC: not available,
skipped, not applicable,

49
00:03:15.275 --> 00:03:18.522
with the interim path and the Learn page.

50
00:03:18.522 --> 00:03:23.220
That result name is one of the seven the
Zero Trust Assessment results kit uses,

51
00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.840
so the same disposition lands
in the closure register.

52
00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.324
The incident check reads incidents
created in the last thirty days, and every

53
00:03:35.324 --> 00:03:37.839
incident still active, with their alerts,

54
00:03:37.839 --> 00:03:40.428
using Learn's list call
with expand alerts.

55
00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:41.827
Each one gets a finding.

56
00:03:41.827 --> 00:03:44.340
Triaged: open, with an owner.

57
00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.678
Untriaged: open, no owner,
but inside its window.

58
00:03:48.678 --> 00:03:53.390
Overdue triage: no owner,
past its window, which fails.

59
00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.252
Closed: resolved with a
classification and a determination.

60
00:03:57.252 --> 00:04:00.577
And closed without
classification, which also fails.

61
00:04:00.577 --> 00:04:04.885
The windows come from the guide's
severity table: one hour for high,

62
00:04:04.885 --> 00:04:08.953
twenty-four for medium, seventy-two
for low and informational.

63
00:04:08.953 --> 00:04:14.280
Containment stays on the response ladder;
the kit only prints each incident's rung.

64
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.080
Who's involved?

65
00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.254
The vulnerability manager
owns the exposure gate:

66
00:04:22.254 --> 00:04:25.655
an owner for every exposed
device, and the weekly queue.

67
00:04:25.655 --> 00:04:28.978
The security operations lead
owns the incident gate:

68
00:04:28.978 --> 00:04:32.755
a daily queue review, and every
overdue incident owned today.

69
00:04:32.755 --> 00:04:35.908
The security officer files
the Exposure Management

70
00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:39.492
disposition in the closure
register, and the inventory gaps.

71
00:04:39.492 --> 00:04:42.505
One caution: the incident
check only reads.

72
00:04:42.505 --> 00:04:46.293
It never assigns, comments
on or closes an incident.

73
00:04:46.293 --> 00:04:49.763
And the severity strings the
exposure query filters on are

74
00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:53.250
as the portal shows them;
check them in the test tenant.

75
00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.100
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

76
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.500
Step 1 runs the exposure check.

77
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.612
Three software rows with nine CVEs
between them, and three exposed devices.

78
00:05:06.612 --> 00:05:07.762
Two have owners.

79
00:05:07.762 --> 00:05:10.493
The third sits in a group no owner covers:

80
00:05:10.493 --> 00:05:14.549
the same device Chapter 2 found
missing from the inventory.

81
00:05:14.549 --> 00:05:16.512
Two findings, one cause.

82
00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:21.302
Step 2 reads the Exposure Management
record: not available in GCC,

83
00:05:21.302 --> 00:05:25.737
skipped, not applicable, and the
interim path in one sentence.

84
00:05:25.737 --> 00:05:29.670
That's what goes in the closure
register, not a failed control.

85
00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.745
Step 3 runs the incident check.

86
00:05:35.745 --> 00:05:37.007
Seven incidents.

87
00:05:37.007 --> 00:05:41.391
Incident 1002, high severity,
has waited three and a half

88
00:05:41.391 --> 00:05:45.583
hours with no owner, against
a one-hour window: overdue.

89
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:50.683
Incident 1005 was resolved with
its classification still unknown.

90
00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:57.320
And incident 1003, medium, has no owner
yet, but it's inside its twenty-four hours.

91
00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:01.571
Step 4 reads the findings as the
security operations lead gets them,

92
00:06:01.571 --> 00:06:03.545
each with its containment rung.

93
00:06:03.545 --> 00:06:05.424
The high one is the gated rung:

94
00:06:05.424 --> 00:06:09.240
the playbook prepares, and
a named approver releases.

95
00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:18.097
Lab 2 uses a test tenant, never production,
with Vulnerability Management data on

96
00:06:18.097 --> 00:06:23.203
at least one device and at least one incident;
the guide's detection test makes one.

97
00:06:23.203 --> 00:06:27.915
Sign in with both read scopes, and run
both checks without the fixture path.

98
00:06:27.915 --> 00:06:30.178
Then look at the raw vulnerability file.

99
00:06:30.178 --> 00:06:34.212
If it's empty in a tenant you know
has high vulnerabilities, the severity

100
00:06:34.212 --> 00:06:38.178
strings differ from the portal's, and
that goes in the verification log.

101
00:06:38.178 --> 00:06:40.860
Nothing changed, so you just sign out.

102
00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:48.260
Here's what these two gates
prove, against NIST's catalog.

103
00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.323
Vulnerabilities by software
and update support

104
00:06:51.323 --> 00:06:54.385
vulnerability monitoring
and flaw remediation.

105
00:06:54.385 --> 00:06:59.273
Every exposed device owned, or a finding,
supports the component inventory.

106
00:06:59.273 --> 00:07:02.020
The Exposure Management
disposition supports

107
00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.698
vulnerability monitoring,
honestly recorded.

108
00:07:04.698 --> 00:07:08.845
Incidents owned inside their windows
and closed with a classification

109
00:07:08.845 --> 00:07:12.647
support incident handling, incident
monitoring, and audit review.

110
00:07:12.647 --> 00:07:16.459
And the incident record with its alerts
supports the reporting decision.

111
00:07:16.459 --> 00:07:19.648
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

112
00:07:19.648 --> 00:07:23.810
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

113
00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:28.960
What breaks in production?

114
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.766
Assuming Exposure Management in GCC
stalls attack-path work, and makes a Zero

115
00:07:33.766 --> 00:07:38.798
Trust Assessment test look failed; the
disposition and the interim path guard it.

116
00:07:38.798 --> 00:07:43.060
An exposed device with no owner
never gets patched; it's a finding.

117
00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.711
Sentinel queries on the
vulnerability tables return nothing,

118
00:07:46.711 --> 00:07:49.660
so the kit queries Defender's
own advanced hunting.

119
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.912
Reading only last month's incidents
misses an old one still open,

120
00:07:53.912 --> 00:07:55.998
so the active filter is read too.

121
00:07:55.998 --> 00:08:00.362
And a time to triage computed from
the wrong field is a precise-looking,

122
00:08:00.362 --> 00:08:03.950
wrong number, so the kit
reports past-window instead.

123
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.050
Here's what done looks like.

124
00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.125
Every device group has an
owner in the owners file.

125
00:08:12.125 --> 00:08:16.325
The Exposure Management disposition
is filed with the closure register.

126
00:08:16.325 --> 00:08:19.150
Every overdue incident has an owner today.

127
00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.863
Every incident closed without a
classification is reopened and classified.

128
00:08:23.863 --> 00:08:27.000
And both run folders are
recorded as evidence.

129
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.443
Next, Chapter 6 keeps the rules that
raise these incidents as files:

130
00:08:31.443 --> 00:08:34.513
tested, and deployed
through Microsoft Graph.

131
00:08:34.513 --> 00:08:35.680
Thanks for watching.
